In the last decade, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have emerged as a new family of nanoporous materials.4 MOFs are assembled by directly bonding metal clusters with organic linkers and thus there is no need to use structure-directing agents as for zeolites. In remarkable contrast to the tetrahedral building blocks in zeolites, MOFs can be synthesized from enormous inorganic clusters (e.g. square-shaped, trigonal, tetrahedral and octahedral) and organic linkers (e.g. carboxylates, imidazolates and tetrazolates). Consequently, MOFs possess a wide range of surface areas (up to $6500\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ and pore sizes (up to $4.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ ), and have been considered as versatile materials for storage, separation, catalysis and biomedical applications.5,6 Fig. 1b shows a prototype MOF (called MOF-5 or IRMOF-1) with internal cage illustrated by the yellow sphere. MOF-5 has a formula of $Z{\mathrm{n}}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ , where BDC is 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate. Each oxide-centered $Z_{\mathrm{{n_{4}O}}}$ tetrahedron in MOF-5 is edge-bridged by six carboxylate linkers resulting in an octahedral $Z_{\mathrm{{n_{4}O}(O_{2}C-)_{6}}}$ building unit, which reticulates into a three-dimensional framework.7 Unlike zeolites, MOFs can be readily functionalized by post-synthesis treatment. To date, tens of thousands of MOFs have been synthesized in this burgeoning field and several (ZIF-8, Cu-BTC and MIL-53) are available commercially.
Apart from zeolites and MOFs, protein crystals are another class of nanoporous materials. They contain solvent-filled pores with various pore sizes $(1.5\mathrm{-}10~\mathrm{\nm})$ , surface areas $(800-2000~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ and porosities $(30\mathrm{-}80\%)$ .8 The pore structures can be tailored by varying protein molecules and crystallization conditions, thus leading to specific functionalities. Even one protein may form different polymorphic crystals depending on temperature, $\mathrm{\pH}$ and additive. For example, lysozyme has tetragonal (see Fig. 1c), orthorhombic, monoclinic and triclinic crystalline structures. In contrast to the strong covalent bonds in zeolites and MOFs, protein crystals are constructed by protein molecules via relatively weaker van der Waals and electrostatic interactions. As such, they are mechanically soft and in the past their applications were limited. However, this limitation has been largely reduced by cross-linking technology. A unique character of protein crystals is the intrinsic chirality because protein molecules are composed of $L$ -amino acids, which create a chiral environment and can discriminate enantiomers. The collective unusual features allow protein crystals to be used for pharmaceutical separation, drug delivery and other potential applications.8
