Zeolites, MOFs and protein crystals represent a wealthy spectrum of nanoporous materials with increasing degree of complexity in building blocks. Table 1 compares their pore sizes, surface areas, building blocks and major applications. In the past, enormous experimental studies have been reported on the synthesis, characterization and applications of different nanoporous materials. As the number of these materials synthesized is extremely large (particularly for MOFs and protein crystals), rationally selecting a suitable material from discovery to utilization is challenging. In this regard, clear microscopic understanding from a molecular level for nanoporous materials is not only of fundamental interest but also practically important. With ever-growing computational resources and advance in mathematical techniques, molecular simulations have become a powerful tool for material science and engineering. Simulations at a molecular level can provide microscopic insights that are experimentally intractable and elucidate underlying physics from the bottom-up. In addition, they can be used to secure fundamental interpretation of experimental observations and establish structure–function relationships based on molecular description.9
In this tutorial review, we summarize the recent simulation studies in zeolites, metal–organic frameworks and protein crystals. Our objective is to provide a comprehensive overview from molecular perspective for energy, environmental and pharmaceutical applications of a wide variety of nanoporous materials. Following this section, simulation methodology is briefly introduced in Section 2. Energy applications are discussed in Section 3 based on the storage of energy carriers $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . In Section 4, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and water purification are presented for environmental applications. In Section 5, drug loading/delivery and chiral separation are illustrated for pharmaceutical applications. Finally, major challenges in future simulation investigation of nanoporous materials are discussed in Section 6.
## 2. Simulation methodology
The underlying information required in simulation is molecular interactions, which exclusively determine the reliability and accuracy of simulation results. Molecular interactions are usually described by a force field consisting of bonded and nonbonded terms, where the bonded term includes bond stretching, bending and torsional interactions; and the nonbonded term has van der Waals and electrostatic interactions. In most force fields, the nonbonded term takes into account only two-body interactions for the sake of computational efficiency. The parameters in a force field can be derived from experimental data or by quantum mechanics (QM) calculations.
With an appropriate force field for molecular interactions, simulation can be performed using either Monte Carlo (MC) or molecular dynamics (MD) method.10,11 MC simulation is a stochastic method to generate representative configurations. One attractive aspect of MC simulation is that only energies rather than forces are evaluated during configurational sampling. MC simulation can be performed with physically unnatural motions, e.g. a jump from one position to the other or random insertion/deletion of a molecule, and thus significantly increasing efficiency. In particular, MC simulation is well-suited to calculate thermodynamic properties for adsorption in nanoporous materials.12,13 For example, commonly used grandcanonical MC (GCMC) simulation with fixed temperature, volume and chemical potential allows adsorbate molecules to exchange between adsorbed phase and bulk fluid reservoir, thus adsorption isotherm can be readily generated. In a canonical ensemble with fixed temperature, volume and number of molecules, Henry’s constant, isosteric heat and binding energy can be estimated by MC simulation.
Following Newton’s second law, MD simulation mimics the natural pathway of molecular motion to sample successive configurations. The initial velocities of molecules at a given temperature are assigned by the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution. At each time, the forces between molecules are calculated, then the equations of motion are solved numerically with a time step, and finally the velocities and positions are updated. The time step is chosen to be sufficiently small so that the total energy of the system is conserved. MD simulation can be performed in an ensemble with a constant volume, temperature or pressure. In addition to thermodynamic properties that can be simulated by MC method, transport properties such as diffusivity and conductivity can be determined from MD trajectory.12,14
In the simulation studies of nanoporous crystalline materials, the coordinates of framework atoms are usually adopted from experimental crystallographic data. Most studies use rigid frameworks with atomic positions fixed. This has two advantages: first, there is no need to evaluate intra-framework interactions; second, the interactions between framework and guest can be pre-tabulated and simulation can be accelerated. Such a simplified treatment is acceptable, particularly for small guest molecules in nanoporous materials. The ordered crystal structures are mimicked using periodic boundary conditions, in which the simulation box is surrounded by its replicated images. In this regard, the simulation system can be considered as infinitely large. Simulation should run for sufficiently long to assure equilibration or steady state is reached, and then ensemble averages can be calculated.
## 3. Energy applications
## 3.1 H2 storage
$\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is an environmentally friendly energy carrier and clean fuel. A major challenge in the practical use of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is to develop an efficient storage system. In 2005, the US Department of Energy (DOE) has set the targets for on-board $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage to be $6.0\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ and $45\ \mathrm{g\L^{-1}}$ by 2010, and $9.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $81\ \mathrm{g~L~}^{-1}$ by 2015 at near-ambient temperature. In 2009, the targets were revised to $4.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $28\ \mathrm{g\L^{-1}}$ by 2010, and $5.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $40\mathrm{~g~L~}^{-1}$ by 2015.15
Extensive experiments have been undertaken over the past decades to examine $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage in a wide variety of nanostructures including activated carbons, carbon nanotubes, zeolites and MOFs.16,17 Molecular simulations have also been used and in turn guide the selection/design of adsorbent materials. Table 2 highlights the representative simulation studies for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption in zeolites and MOFs. An early simulation study was reported by Darkrim et al. to investigate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption in NaA zeolite at room temperature and high pressures.18 The simulation incorporated the polarization effects of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules induced by the electric field of zeolite. The maximum adsorption was predicted to be $2\mathrm{-}2.5\ \mathrm{~H}_{2}$ molecules per $\alpha$ -cage and the computed isotherms agreed quantitatively well with measured data. This study proposed a new set of potential parameters for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -zeolite interactions and suggested that the parameters of Na and O atoms have a strong influence on computed adsorption. A similar study was reported by Vitillo et al. on the progressive filling of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in 12 siliceous zeolites.19 The force field used was tested by comparing the optimized crystalline structures of MFI and CHA with experimental measurements. They found the flexible non-pentasils zeolites display the highest maximal capacity in the range of $2.65{-}2.86~\mathrm{~wt\%}$ . An interesting side-product of this study was the prediction of a scale for zeolite flexibility, which might be useful in the design of materials for gas separation and catalysis.
Because of the narrow pores and small surface areas, $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity in zeolites is far below the targets for automotive applications. With the development of highly porous MOFs in the last decade, several research groups have conducted simulation studies for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption in MOFs. Yang and Zhong performed GCMC simulation for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption in isoreticular MOFs (IRMOF-1, IRMOF-8 and IRMOF-18).21 They showed that metal–oxide clusters are the preferential adsorption sites for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and the effect of organic linkers becomes evident with increasing pressure. In addition, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage capacity in IRMOFs was found to be similar to that in carbon nanotubes but higher than in zeolites. The study suggested that although IRMOFs are promising for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage, their suitability for practical use should be evaluated further at room temperature. Frost et al. simulated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption in 10 IRMOFs possessing same framework topology and surface chemistry.22 The predicted adsorption isotherms were in acceptable agreement with available experimental results. The results revealed the existence of three adsorption regimes. As shown in Fig. 2, at a low pressure (0.1 bar), the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake correlates with the heat of adsorption; at an intermediate pressure $30\ \mathrm{bar})$ , the uptake correlates with the surface area; and at a high pressure (100 bar), the uptake correlates with the free volume. The accessible surface area and free volume calculated from crystallographic data were used to evaluate the potential of IRMOFs to meet gravimetric and volumetric targets for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage.
Most simulation studies listed in Table 2 indicate that $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage capacities in MOFs are higher than in zeolites, and the DOE targets can be achieved at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ but not at room temperature. Such a trend is also observed in experimental studies for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption in MOFs.17 To enhance capacity, a few strategies have been proposed such as introducing catenated frameworks, open metal sites, light metals or functional linkers. For example, Ryan et al. simulated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption in catenated IRMOFs and demonstrated that catenation can be beneficial for improving $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage at cryogenic temperatures and low pressures, but not necessarily at room temperature and high pressures.25 They suggested that one should be cautious in extrapolating from results at low temperatures and pressures to higher ones.
Combining QM derived force field and GCMC simulation, Han et al. examined $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage in Li-doped MOFs.26 The predicted results at $77~\mathrm{K}$ match well with measured data and Li-doped MOFs exhibit larger capacity than non-doped counterparts. Specifically, Li-MOF-C30 adsorbs $3.89~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at 20 bar and reaches up to $4.56~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 50 bar, almost an order of magnitude greater than in MOF-C30. In a related study, Cao et al. predicted $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption in Li-doped covalent-organic frameworks (COFs).28 With light elements and low framework densities, COFs are attractive for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage. The authors found the capacity in Li-doped COF-105 is $4.25\mathrm{\wt\%}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 100 bar, one of the highest reported in the literature. Alternatively, $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption was simulated in Li-alkoxide functionalized IRMOF-8 and -14 by Klontzas et al.29 This type of functionalization differs from metal doping: the metal atoms are not bound onto the aromatic rings of MOFs; consequently, the aromatic rings remain free and can adsorb $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules. They calculated the interaction energies between $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules and Li-alkoxide groups and found the energies were three times larger compared with unmodified MOFs. Consequently, a high capacity of $4.5\mathrm{\wt\%}$ was predicted at $300\mathrm{~K~}$ and 100 bar. These studies imply that metal-doping and metal-alkoxide functionalization are effective approaches to enhance $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage in MOFs.
Experiments have demonstrated that ionic MOFs, possessing charged framework and charge-balancing nonframework ions, provide strong electric fields and high affinity for guest molecules.17 In this regard, Jiang reported a simulation study for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption in a cationic MOF with soc topology.31 The soc-MOF is assembled by oxygen-centered indiumcarboxylate trimers $[\mathrm{In}_{3}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{6}]$ . The cationic framework is charge-balanced by nonframework $\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$ ions. The adsorption sites of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in soc-MOF identified from simulation are consistent with those by inelastic neutron scattering measurements. The predicted $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ isotherm agrees well with experimental data, reaching $2.61~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $78\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1.2 atm. Such a high capacity is rarely seen in other MOFs. Jiang and coworkers further simulated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption in a highly porous cationic rht-MOF.32 Three types of open cages exist in rht-MOF: rhombicuboctahedral, tetrahedral and cuboctahedral cages. $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ isotherm predicted at $77~\mathrm{K}$ is in accord with experiment. The $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capacity was estimated to be $2.4~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 1 bar and $6.2\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ at 50 bar, among the highest reported in MOFs. Therefore, using ionic frameworks is another strategy to enhance $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption and deserves further investigation.
The simulation studies discussed above, along with experimental measurements,17 show that $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage capacities in several MOFs/COFs achieve the DOE targets; however, they were evaluated at cryogenic $77~\mathrm{K}$ . At room temperature, the capacities drop significantly and fall below the targets. In addition, the gravimetric DOE targets refer to the entire storage system, which includes all hardware (e.g. tank, valves, cooling or heating systems). Consequently, actual $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage capacity should be higher and breakthrough is required to further enhance $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -powered automobiles.
## 3.2 $\mathbf{CH_{4}}$ storage
$\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is an alternative fuel as it has much lower carbon emissions compared with traditional fossil fuels. The target of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage is $180\ \mathrm{v}(\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{v}$ (the volume of gas adsorbed at standard temperature and pressure per volume of the storage vessel) at ambient temperature and 35 bar, which is the energy density of adsorbed $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ approximately equal to that of compressed natural gas.33
Experimental studies for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage in nanoporous materials have been reviewed.34 Table 3 lists the representative simulation studies at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 35 bar. Among these, Du¨ ren et al. examined the adsorption characteristics of IRMOFs and compared them with MFI, FAU zeolites and MCM-41.35 From GCMC simulation, they found a good correlation between surface area and adsorption capacity at $298{\mathrm{~K~}}$ and 35 bar, and suggested that an ideal adsorbent for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage should have a high pore volume, a large surface area, a strong adsorption affinity and a low framework density. They also proposed a not-yet synthesized IRMOF showing a larger adsorption capacity than other IRMOFs. A comparative simulation study was reported by Babarao et al. for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption in three typical nanoporous materials (IRMOF-1, MFI and $\mathrm{C}_{168}$ schwarzite).36 As mentioned earlier, IRMOF-1 and MFI are prototype MOF and zeolite, respectively. $\mathrm{C_{168}}$ schwarzite is a hypothetical structure mimicking a nanoporous carbon. The simulated isotherms and isosteric heats of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in IRMOF-1 and MFI match closely with available experimental data. The study reveals that IRMOF-1 has the largest capacity for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ among the three nano-structures and suggests that the capacity in IRMOF-1 can be further improved by appropriately tuning organic linkers. This is reflected in a simulation study by Wang, in which $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption was examined in various MOFs including IRMOFs-1, -6, -8, -10 and -14, $\mathrm{Cu}$ -BTC, CPL-28, CPL-552 and $\mathrm{Cu(AF_{6})(B P Y)_{2}}$ $(\mathbf{A}=\mathbf{Si}/\mathbf{Ge})$ .37 The results show that high isosteric heat, large surface area/free volume and low density of framework are all desirable characteristics for a material with high adsorption capacity. However, not all these factors are compatible and there exists a complex interplay influencing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacity. Interestingly, similar to the correlations in Fig. 2 for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption, good correlations were found between the isosteric heat at a low pressure and the pore size, as well as between the capacity at a high pressure and the surface area/free volume. These molecular-based correlations can be used to predict adsorption capacity and screen MOFs for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage.
To enhance $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage capacity in MOFs, strategies such as adding functional groups, light metals or open metal sites have been proposed. Jhon et al. reported a simulation study for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption in alkoxy-functionalized IRMOFs.39 They found the constricted pores induced by alkoxy groups enhance $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption at low and moderate pressures due to the stronger potential overlap of smaller pores. However, the saturation capacity is reduced at high pressures as the free volume decreases. Combining QM and GCMC methods, Lan et al. investigated $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption in COF-102, COF-103 and their Li-doped compounds.42 The predicted capacities in COF-102 and COF-103 agree well with experimentally measured data. A high capacity up to $303~\mathrm{\v(STP)/v}$ was predicted for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption in Li-doped COFs, nearly twice that in non-doped counterparts. This enhancement was attributed to strong affinity of doped Li atoms for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . In a separate study, the effect of open metal sites on $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption was examined in $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dhtp})$ (open metal $\mathbf{M}=Z\mathbf{n} $ , Mg, Mn, Co, Ni; dhtp $\mathbf{\varepsilon}=2{,}5.$ - dihydroxyterephthalate) by Wu et al. using neutron diffraction and QM computation.43 The $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dhtp})$ compounds possess exceptionally high density of open metal sites, which are the primary adsorption sites for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ as determined from neutron diffraction and illustrated in Fig. 3. The experimentally measured isosteric heats and QM calculated binding energies both indicate that $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ affinity to the open metals is significantly stronger than that on typical adsorption sites in classical MOFs. The authors attributed this to the electrostatic interactions between $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and the unsaturated metal ions. Among the five isostructural MOFs studied, $\mathrm{{Ni}}_{2}(\mathrm{{dhtp})}$ was found to exhibit the highest $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacity of approximately $200\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ .
The simulation studies listed in Table 3 indicate that the target of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage can be reached in COFs and modified MOFs/COFs. Nevertheless, the theoretical predictions need to be validated by experiments. Among the different strategies to enhance $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage capacity, framework functionalization may not be effective at high pressures. In contrast, metal doping and introducing open metal sites appear to be more attractive.
It is interesting to note that alkanes are the major components of gasoline and a number of simulation studies have been reported for their adsorption in zeolites and MOFs. For example, Calero et al. simulated the adsorption of linear alkanes $({\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{-}{\bf C}_{10})$ in $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ -exchanged zeolite FAU (faujasite) using a united-atom force field.44 The force field successfully reproduces experimental adsorption properties over a wide range of temperatures, pressures and $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ densities. It also reproduces the $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ positions in FAU known from crystallography. The applicability of the force field is not limited to low pressures and pure adsorbates, as it also reproduces the separation of binary mixtures at high pressures. Alternatively, the adsorption of linear and branched alkanes $\displaystyle(\mathbf{C}_{1}\mathrm{-}\mathbf{C}_{5})$ in IRMOF-1 was simulated by Jiang and Sandler.45 A long alkane is preferentially adsorbed over a shorter one at low pressures, whereas the reverse is found at high pressures. In addition, a linear isomer adsorbs more than its branched analogue as a result of configurational entropy. This study also indicates that the extent of alkane adsorption in IRMOF-1 is greater than in silicalite and carbon nanotube.
## 4. Environmental applications
## 4.1 $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture
The atmospheric $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration has increased from 280 to $385~\mathrm{ppm}$ since the industrial revolution, largely due to fossilfuel combustion and deforestation.46 It is generally believed that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ emissions have caused the rise of global surface temperature by $0.74~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ over the past century. Currently, carbon capture and sequestration (CCS) is not only a scientific interest, but a societal issue for environmental protection. As a key step in CCS, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is required to be captured in a post- or pre-combustion process. A post-combustion capture involves the separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from exhaust flue gas primarily consisting of $\Nu_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . In a pre-combustion capture, the partial oxidation of feedstock fuel produces syngas (CO and $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{H}_{2}\rangle}\end{array}$ , which reacts with steam yielding shifted syngas ( $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2})$ ; $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is then separated from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ mixture. On the other hand, the separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ mixture is important for natural gas sweetening because impurities such as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduce the calorie content of natural gas and need to be separated.
Several approaches have been proposed for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture including cryogenic distillation, amine scrubbing, membrane separation and sorbent adsorption. Because of the occurrence of phase transition, cryogenic distillation is energetically intensive. Amine scrubbing has deficiency in solvent regeneration and equipment corrosion. Membrane separation is restricted by permeability-selectivity trade off. Upon comparison, adsorption in porous materials has considerably high energy efficiency, low capital cost, large separation capability and ease for scaling-up. To achieve efficient adsorption-based $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, the selection of an appropriate adsorbent is crucial. A large number of experiments have been carried out for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture in numerous nanoporous adsorbents.47
Table 4 summarizes the recent simulation studies for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture in zeolites and MOFs at room temperature. For example, Goj et al. reported the separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ mixture in three siliceous zeolites with identical chemical composition but differing pore structures (MFI, ITQ-3 and ITQ-7).48 The predicted adsorption isotherms of pure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ agree well with available experimental data in MFI. $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivities in the three zeolites vary strongly with the crystal structure. The study indicates that the electric fields of different strengths present in zeolites lead to the observed selectivities and suggests the appropriate selection of zeolite structure is important for the separation of flue gas. Jiang and Sandler further simulated the adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ mixture in $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ -exchanged ZSM-5.49 With experimentally validated potentials, the predicted selectivity in Na-ZSM-5 is significantly higher than in siliceous ZSM-5 and increases with increasing density of $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ ions. This is because $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ has a large quadrupolar moment and interacts strongly with nonframework $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ ions. As discussed below, similar behavior was also observed in cation-exchanged MOFs.
A comparative study was carried out by Liu and Smit on the performance of zeolites and MOFs for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture.50 They selected MFI with intersecting channels, DDR and LTA with cages, and seven MOFs (Cu-BTC, MIL-47, IRMOF-1, -11, -12, -13 and -14) with a wide range of chemical compositions, pore sizes and topologies. The simulation results show that although MOFs outperform over zeolites for gas storage, their separation performance is comparable to zeolites. The catenated IRMOFs exhibit a slightly higher selectivity than non-catenated counterparts. Also, they suggested that the difference in the quadrupole moments of sorbates (e.g. between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{N}_{2}^{\cdot\cdot}$ ) should be considered in selecting a suitable material for separation. Zhong and coworkers performed a simulation study on the separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ mixture in Cu-BTC.51 They found the electrostatic interactions (EI) between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and framework have a large effect on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity as shown in Fig. 4a. Fig. 4b illustrates that both adsorbate molecules are mainly located in the octahedral pockets of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC. Due to a larger quadrupole moment, however, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules are bound closer to the $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ atoms than ${\bf N}_{2}$ . They further predicted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separation in eight IRMOFs functionalized by $-\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ , $-\mathrm{F}$ , $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups.54 The selectivity was found to enhance slightly as a consequence of increased interactions between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and functional groups.
Therefore, both catenated and functionalized MOFs exhibit only slight enhancement for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture.50,54 As an alternative, three ionic MOFs (soc-MOF, rht-MOF and rho-ZMOF) have been examined by Jiang and coworkers for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation.31,32,55 In all the three ionic MOFs, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is more predominantly adsorbed than $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ because $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ interacts strongly with the nonframework ions. Fig. 5a shows the density contours of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules and $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ ions for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ mixture in Na-rho-ZMOF. It is clearly observed that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules are strongly adsorbed onto $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ ions. The predicted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity shown in Fig. 5b is significantly higher than in most MOFs. By switching off the charges on the framework and ions, the selectivity was found to decrease substantially. This indicates the important role of electrostatic interactions in the observed high selectivity in ionic MOFs. Particularly noteworthy is the selectivity behaves differently in the three ionic MOFs with different free volumes and charge densities. The fractional free volume increases in the order of $r h o\mathrm{-}Z\mathbf{MOF}<s o c\mathbf{-}\mathbf{MOF}<r h t\mathbf{-}\mathbf{MOF}$ , but the charge density increases in the opposite order. Consequently, rho-ZMOF has the smallest porosity and largest charge density, and thus exhibits the highest selectivity, followed by soc-MOF and rht-MOF. Together with early discussion, ionic MOFs are demonstrated to be good candidates for gas storage and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture. However, experimental confirmation is required to further ascertain their performance.
While the simulation studies discussed above for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture were focused on adsorption-based separation, Krishna et al. investigated the performance of 12 zeolites with varying topologies for membrane-based separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ mixture.59 The zeolites considered consist of onedimensional channels (MOR, TON, FER, LTL), intersecting channels (MFI, ISV and BEA) and cages (FAU, LTA, CHA and DDR). The adsorption selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ mixture was first calculated and the 12 zeolites were narrowed down to MOR, LTL, MFI, CHA and DDR. The diffusion selectivity was then predicted, CHA and DDR were found to exhibit higher selectivity. The permeation selectivity was finally estimated, CHA and DDR were identified as suitable materials for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separation.
Keskin and Sholl evaluated the performance of chemically diverse MOFs (IRMOF-8, -9, -10, -14 and $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathbf{BDC})(\mathrm{TED})_{0.5})$ for membrane-based separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ mixtures.60 They used atomistic models to predict the adsorption and diffusion properties of gas mixtures and then membrane selectivities. In all the MOFs examined, the membrane separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ or $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is not favorable due to low $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selectivities. This work highlights the fact that understanding membrane-based separation requires a detailed analysis of both molecular adsorption and diffusion. In addition, mixture effects play a crucial role in determining the performance and examining only single-component gases is insufficient. Recently, Sholl and coworkers proposed a computational tool to identify MOFs for kinetic separation by calculating the difference between the diffusion coefficients of gas species.61 A geometric approach was introduced to rapidly identify the key features of a pore structure that control molecular diffusion and couple this with modeling calculations to predict the Henry’s constant and diffusion activation energy for a range of spherical adsorbates. They demonstrated the approach for over 500 MOFs and 160 siliceous zeolites. The results indicate that a significant number of materials are predicted to have extraordinary properties for gas separation. Fig. 6 shows that 101 MOFs with the pore-limiting diameters between 2 and $4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ are potentially useful for the kinetic separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ mixture.
## 4.2 Water purification
A substantial amount of contaminants such as heavy metal ions ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ and $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ have been introduced into water. These ions are toxic and carcinogenic even at very low concentrations. They can accumulate in living organisms and cause lethal effects on the human body. With increasing population, the demand for high-quality water increases. It is important to remove these metal ions from industrial, agricultural and municipal wastewater, thus minimizing health and environmental risks. A technologically feasible and cost-effective approach to remove metal ions is through ion exchange with cations in porous materials.
A handful of simulation studies have been reported on the structural and dynamic properties of cations and water in zeolites toward the understanding of ion exchange process. Murad et al. simulated the dynamics and energetics of ion exchange between $\mathrm{LiCl/CaCl}_{2}$ solution and NaA zeolite membrane.62 The results show that with appropriate driving forces, ion exchange can be clearly observed. The process is primarily energetically driven and entropic forces do not appear to dominate. In addition to clarifying the molecular basis for ion exchange, this study is potentially useful to determine the hydrodynamic parameters for ion exchange at a molecular level. Nalaparaju et al. performed simulation for the exchange of $\mathbf{Pb}^{2^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ with $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ ions in Na-ETS-10 membrane.63 The residence times, mean-squared displacements and radial distribution functions of the ions were determined to characterize their dynamic and structural properties. It was found that most $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ ions reside in the membrane and have small mobility. However, $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions frequently exchange with their counterparts outside the membrane and exhibit relatively larger mobility. The exchange capability of Na-ETS-10 for the three metal ions follows the order of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}>\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}>\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , which is consistent with experimental observation.
To the best of our knowledge, no simulation study has been reported in the literature for water purification in MOFs. Only recently, Hu and Jiang investigated the desalination of NaCl solution through a ZIF-8 membrane by MD simulation. In contrast to many MOFs, ZIF-8 is chemically/thermally stable in water and organic solvents, and has received considerable interest for its potential applications. As shown in Fig. 7, the simulation system consists of three compartments including NaCl solution, ZIF-8 membrane and pure water bath; and an external pressure is exerted to mimic a reverse osmosis process. Because of the sieving effect of small apertures in ZIF-8, $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ ions cannot transport through the membrane and water desalination was observed. The flux of water permeating the membrane was found to scale linearly with the external pressure. This study suggests that ZIF-8 might be an appealing membrane material for water purification.
In addition to metal ions, organic compounds (e.g. alcohols) are also required to be removed for water purification. For this purpose, Lu et al. performed simulation for the separation of water–ethanol mixture in zeolites (MFI, MOR, CFI and DON) at ambient temperature and pressure. The results show water is more dominantly adsorbed in zeolites than ethanol and the separation performance depends on the pore size and framework of zeolite.64 Currently, only a few simulation studies have been reported for the separation of water– alcohols in MOFs. Chen et al. combined experimental technique and simulation to examine the adsorption of water and methanol in hydrophobic $\mathrm{Zn(BDC)(TED)_{0.5}}$ .65 The simulated isotherms are in fairly good agreement with experimental data. While water adsorption in $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{BDC})(\mathrm{TED})_{0.5}$ is vanishingly small, methanol shows much stronger adsorption. The MOF exhibits high methanol–water selectivity up to 20 and is potentially useful for water purification. Nalaparaju et al. simulated the adsorption of water and alcohols (methanol and ethanol) in hydrophilic Na-rho-ZMOF and hydrophobic ZIF-71.66 Both MOFs have rho topology and comparable pore size. In Na-rho-ZMOF, water adsorption increases continuously with increasing pressure and competitively replaces alcohol at high pressures. In ZIF-71, alcohol is more preferentially adsorbed at low pressures, but surpassed by water with increasing pressure. They found that the framework charges have a substantial effect on the adsorption in Na-rho-ZMOF but not in ZIF-71. This study provides in-depth molecular understanding for the adsorption of water and alcohols in the two MOFs with identical topology and reveals the significantly different adsorption mechanisms.
Compared with gas storage and separation, simulation studies reported for water purification in nanoporous materials are far fewer. This is primarily due to the significant amount of computational time required to simulate dense liquid phase. Consequently, the microscopic understanding of liquid phase separation is less complete. With increasing computational power, however, we expect more simulation studies will be reported in this area and a deeper molecular insight will be provided.
## 5. Pharmaceutical applications
## 5.1 Drug loading/delivery
The interest in using nanoporous materials for drug loading/ delivery has increased considerably. The loading capacity and release of drug in a porous carrier are governed by a variety of factors including pore size, shape, connectivity and host affinity. In traditional materials such as inorganic silica, carbonaceous materials and polymeric matrixes, drug loading capacity is usually not sufficiently high and encapsulated drug is difficult to be released specifically. To achieve a high loading and a controlled release, porous materials possessing large volumes and regular structures are desired.
Mesoporous MOFs with well defined pores provide a wealth of opportunities for drug delivery. Currently, the microscopic understanding of drug-MOF interaction remains elusive and molecular-level study is crucial for the development of MOFs as novel drug devices with ideal loading and specific delivery. Horcajada et al. performed QM calculation to examine the interaction of ibuprofen (IBU) with MIL-53(Fe).67 A strong binding energy of $57\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ was found, which is in the same magnitude for IBU interacting with zeolites. They observed the formation of a strong hydrogen bond between the carboxylic oxygen of IBU and the hydroxyl group of MIL-53(Fe). The spatial conformation of IBU in the pores of MIL-53(Fe) is slightly changed from that in vacuum. The predicted interaction and geometry of IBU in MIL-53(Fe) are consistent with experimental measurements.
Babarao and Jiang reported a computational study on the energetics and dynamics of IBU in two host carriers, MIL-101 and UMCM-1.68 Both MOFs possess large pore volumes $(\sim2~\mathrm{m}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and high surface areas $(\sim4800\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ . MIL-101 contains unsaturated Lewis acidic $\mathrm{Cr}$ sites by removing terminal waters in octahedral $\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{O}$ trimers. In contrast, no exposed metal sites exist in UMCM-1. The predicted maximum loading of IBU in MIL-101 is $1.11\textrm{g}\mathrm{IBU}\textrm{g}^{-1}$ MIL-101, which agrees fairly well with experimentally determined loading of 1.37. They used MC simulation and QM calculation to determine the conformations of IBU near the metal oxides in MIL-101 and UMCM-1. As shown in Fig. 8, a coordination bond is formed between the carboxylic oxygen of IBU and the $\mathrm{Cr}$ site in MIL-101. However, no such bond is formed with UMCM-1 due to the absence of unsaturated metal sites. The mobility of IBU in MIL-101 is substantially smaller than in UMCM-1. These factors contribute to the delayed release of IBU in MIL-101 as experimentally observed.
It should be noted that the experimental studies of IBU loading/delivery in MIL-53(Fe) and MIL-101 were conducted with an organic solvent (hexane). However, solvent was not included in the above two computational studies.67,68 The existence of solvent may screen the interaction and reduce the accessibility between drug and carrier. Practically, the selection of solvent depends on the polarity of drug. A polar solvent is usually used to promote the loading of a polar drug, and vice versa. More elaborate investigation on drug behavior in MOFs should take into account solvent effect.
## 5.2 Chiral separation
In Mother Nature, living organisms exhibit significantly different biological responses to chiral enantiomers. One enantiomer may have desired pharmacological activity, while the other is inactive or toxic. The production of pure enantiomeric compounds is of central importance for pharmaceutical industry. However, the separation of chiral molecules is challenging since a pair of enantiomers display identical physical and chemical properties. Only in asymmetric environment (e.g. chiral nanoporous materials), enantiomers experience different affinities and may be separated.
Few simulation studies have been reported for chiral separation in nanoporous materials. Clark et al. simulated the adsorption of chiral enantiomers in heterochiral UCSB-7K and homochiral zeolite beta polymorph A (BEA).69 The enantiomeric excess was predicted to achieve $40\text{\textperthousand}$ in UCSB-7K and $10\%$ in BEA. The chiral selectivity in microporous solids was found to be sensitive to the shape and size matching between adsorbate and adsorbent. Fig. 9 illustrates the atomic-siting of dimethyldiphenylallene enantiomers in the left-handed BEA polymorph A, showing that two enantiomers have distinctly different orientations. This study implies it may be possible to use selective chiral adsorption to block the micropores of a specific chirality and thus produce a chiral porous material.
Recently, Zhang and Jiang investigated the enantioselective adsorption and diffusion of $S{-}/R$ -glycidol enantiomers in SU-32.70 SU-32 are the first example of enantiopure homochiral zeolites and can be synthesized in either right- (SU-32a) or left-handed (SU-32b). These rare chiral zeolites provide beautiful benchmark structures to examine the microscopic properties of chiral molecules in single-handed helical channels. They found S-glycidol is more preferentially adsorbed than $R$ -glycidol in SU-32a, and the opposite is observed in SU-32b. The enantiomeric excess of $S{-}/R$ -glycidol racemic mixture is up to $25\%$ . For pure enantiomers, S-glycidol diffuses faster in SU-32a and slower in SU-32b, whereas the reverse is true for $R$ -glycidol. The symmetries in adsorption and diffusion are well present in the two zeolites. The simulation results suggest that enantiopure SU-32a and SU-32b have distinct enantioselectivity for glycidol enantiomers, particularly the selective adsorption, and might be useful for chiral separation.
With the synthesis of chiral MOFs in the last decade, only recently were two simulation studies reported by Snurr and coworkers for enantioselective separation in chiral MOFs. They simulated the adsorption of chiral hydrocarbons: (R,S)-1,3-dimethylallene, $(R,S)$ -1,2-dimethylcyclobutane and (R,S)-1,2-dimethylcyclopropane in homochiral MOFs consisting of cadmium centers and BINOL-type linkers.71 The small zig-zag channels in the chiral MOFs largely contribute to most of the enantioselectivity, but the larger helical channels show no contribution. This reveals that a proper fitting of adsorbate inside a chiral framework is crucial to enantioselectivity. They further examined the collective effects of multiple chiral selectors on the enantioselective adsorption of 1,3-dimethylallene racemic mixture in three homochiral MOFs with the same linkers but different topologies.72 The presence of closely packed chiral selectors in the MOFs was found to enhance the enantioselectivity due to confinement. The authors suggested that the closely situated chiral selectors function synergistically to enhance enantioselectivity, which can be tailored by modifying the molecular composition of individual chiral selectors.
Protein crystals have been experimentally tested in liquid chromatography for the separation of drug or enantiomer mixtures.8 Molecular-level study on the separation mechanism is scarce, but desirable for in-depth microscopic understanding. Toward this end, Hu and Jiang examined the separation of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{D}/\mathrm{L}$ -phenylglycine in a thermolysin crystal by MD simulation, in which thermolysin crystal acts as the stationary phase and water as the mobile phase.73 As shown in Fig. 10, L-phenylglycine transports faster than D-phenylglycine, in accord with experimentally observed elution order. The chiral discrimination of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{D/L}}$ -phenylglycines was attributed to the collective contributions of chiral centers in thermolysin. From energetic and structural analysis, D-phenylglycine was found to interact more strongly with thermolysin than L-phenylglycine; consequently, it stays more proximally to thermolysin for a longer time. In addition, they also simulated the separation of amino acids (Arg, Phe and Trp) in a glucose isomerase crystal.74 The simulation results show the transport velocity of amino acids decreases in the order of $\mathrm{Arg}>\mathrm{Phe}>\mathrm{Trp}$ , consistent with experimental measurements. It was observed that the elution order is not affected by solute concentration or the flowing rate of mobile phase. Arg is highly hydrophilic and charged, interacts with water (the mobile phase) more strongly compared with Phe and Trp; consequently, Arg moves faster with the mobile phase. The simulation reveals that the separation mechanism of amino acids in protein crystal is due to the counterbalance between solute–protein and solute–water interactions.
It is instructive to point out that the simulation models in the above two studies73,74 did not incorporate cross-linking agent glutaraldehyde. As mentioned earlier, protein molecules interact via van der Waals and electrostatic forces to form mechanically soft crystals. In experimental testing, glutaraldehyde was used to cross-link protein molecules to enhance their rigidity and stability. The absence of cross-linker in the simulation may overestimate the transport velocities of analytes. Nevertheless, the main features of the liquid chromatographic separation process have been captured by the simulation and the predicted elution order is consistent with experimental observation.
## 6. Summary and challenges
This tutorial review summarizes the recent molecular simulation studies in nanoporous materials ranging from zeolites, MOFs to protein crystals for energy, environmental and pharmaceutical applications. Specifically, energy applications are discussed in terms of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and water purification are described for environmental applications, and drug loading/delivery and chiral separation are illustrated for pharmaceutical applications. As we can see, molecular simulations provide atomic-resolution and time-resolved insights, which are essential in securing quantitative interpretation of experimental results. With the microscopic understanding, nanoporous materials can be screened and designed for practical applications without resort to timeconsuming experiments and thus expediting material and process design.
In conclusion, molecular simulations have been playing an increasingly important role in material science and engineering. Considerable progress has been achieved from simulations to establish quantitative structure–function relationships for nanoporous materials. On the other hand, the major challenges outlined above provide new opportunities and avenues. Synergistically integrated with cutting-edge experiments, molecular-level studies will continue to contribute to our in-depth knowledge for the development of novel nanoporous materials for new applications in emerging areas.
